===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*lewk-}}
From {{der|en|frm|lustre|}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|lustro}}, from {{der|en|roa-oit|lustrare||brighten}}, from {{der|en|la|lūstrō||to purify, to brighten}}, from {{der|en|la|lūstrum||purification ritual}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}} {{lb|en|American spelling}}

# The [[ability]] or [[condition]] of [[shining]] with [[reflect]]ed [[light]]; [[sheen]], [[gleam]], [[gloss]], [[sparkle]], [[shine]], etc.
#: {{co|en|metallic '''luster'''}}
#: {{co|en|pearly '''luster'''}}
#: {{co|en|the diamond’s '''luster'''}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=V|canto=11|footer=Indianapolis: Hackett, 2006, p. 162|text=And over all the fields themselves did muster,<br>With bils and glayves making a dreadfull '''luster''';<br>That forst at first those knights backe to retyre:<br>As when the wrathfull ''Boreas'' doth bluster,<br>Nought may abide the tempest of his yre,<br>Both man and beast doe fly, and succour doe inquyre.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|III|VII|text=''First Servant:'' O, I am slain! My lord, you have one eye left<br>To see some mischief on him. O! ''[Dies.''<br>''Cornwall:'' Lest it see more, prevent it. Out, vile jelly!<br>Where is thy '''lustre''' now?<br>''Gloucester:'' All dark and comfortless.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1810|author=w:William Blake|title=[[w:Milton: A Poem in Two Books|Milton: A Poem in Two Books]]|section=Book I, 1-5
|passage=Daughters of Beulah! Muses who inspire the Poet’s Song!<br>Record the journey of immortal Milton through your realms<br>Of terror & mild moony '''lustre''', in soft sexual delusions<br>Of varied beauty, to delight the wanderer and repose<br>His burning thirst & freezing hunger! {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Eddison Worm|105|chapter=8
|passage=The canopy above the bed was a mosaic of tiny stones, jet, serpentine, dark hyacinth, black marble, bloodstone, and lapis lazuli, so confounded in a maze of altering hue and '''lustre''' that they might mock the palpitating sky of night.}}
#* '''2001''', James Wood, Introduction to [[w:Saul Bellow|Saul Bellow]], ''Collected Stories'', New York: Viking, p. xvii,
#*: Curiously enough, the stream of consciousness, for all its reputation as the great accelerator of description, actually slows down realism, asks it to dawdle over tiny remembrances, tiny details and '''lusters''', to circle and return.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[shining|Shining]] [[light]] from within, [[luminosity]], [[brightness]], [[shine]].
#: {{co|en|the sun’s '''luster'''}}
#: {{co|en|the '''luster''' of the minor stars}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IV|line=846-850|text={{...}} abashed the devil stood,<br>And felt how awful goodness is, and saw<br>Virtue in her shape how lovely, saw, and pined<br>His loss; but chiefly to find here observed<br>His '''lustre''' visibly impaired; yet seemed<br>Undaunted. {{...}}|year=1873}}
#* '''1717''', {{w|Joseph Addison}}, ''Metamorphoses'' Book III, The Story of Cadmus, [http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10587/pg10587-images.html]
#*: {{quote|en|The scorching sun was mounted high, / In all its '''lustre''', to the noonday sky.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[shining|Shining]] [[beauty]], [[splendor]], [[attractiveness]] or [[attraction]].
#* {{RQ:Addison Cato|i|2|How does the '''Luſtre''' of our Father’s Actions,<br>Through the dark Cloud of Ills that cover him,<br>Break out, and burn with more triumphant Brightneſs!}}
#: {{ux|en|After so many years in the same field, the job had lost its '''luster'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1730|author=James Thomson|title=Autumn|journal=Seasons|section=186
|passage=When Autumn's yellow '''lustre''' gilds the world...}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1836|author=w:Oliver Wendell Holmes|chapter=Poetry: A Metrical Essay|title=The Poetical Works of Oliver Wendell Holmes in Two Volumes: Volume I|location=Boston & New York|publisher=Houghton Mifflin|year_published=1892|page=37|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=F24aAAAAMAAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=Thus err the many, who, entranced to find<br>Unwonted '''lustre''' in some clearer mind,<br>Believe that Genius sets the laws at naught<br>Which chain the pinions of our wildest thought;}}
#* '''1970''', [[w:Shmuel Yosef Agnon|S.Y. Agnon]], "Agunot" in ''Twenty-One Stories'', New York: Schocken Books, p. 30,
#*: Their days of rest are wrested from them, their feasts are fasts, their lot is dust instead of '''luster'''.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[shining|Shining]] [[fame]], [[renown]], [[glory]].
#: {{ux|en|After the scandal, the idol lost his '''luster''' and could only get work in [[Vegas]].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1895|title=The Gentleman's Magazine|volume=279|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=2FpIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA602&lpg=PA602&dq=%22wotton%22+%22rather+without+obscurity%22&source=bl&ots=opQShsDL-j&sig=CgnVi4CVrgnDZZQN_CbvTLUic5E&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwj4zpyOm-7MAhUG2WMKHQ2OAQAQ6AEIHTAA#v=onepage&q=%22wotton%22%20%22rather%20without%20obscurity%22&f=false|page=602
|passage={{...}} whose ancestors, says Clarendon, had been transported out of Normandy with the Conqueror, "and had continued," says Sir Henry Wotton, "about the space of four hundred years, rather without obscurity than with any great '''lustre''' {{...}}".}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1971|author=w:Cynthia Ozick|title=The Butterfly and the Traffic Light|journal=Collected Stories|location=London|publisher=Weidenfeld & Nicolson|year_published=2006|page=288
|passage=But Main, High, and Central have no past; rather, their past is now. It is not the fault of the inhabitants that nothing has gone before them. Nor are they to be condemned if they make their spinal streets conspicuous, and confer egregious '''lustre''' and false acclaim on Central, High, or Main, and erect minarets and marquees indeed as though their city were already in dream and fable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Florence Tamagne|title=A History of Homosexuality in Europe, Volume I & II: Berlin, London, Paris, 1919-1939|location=New York|publisher=Algora|page=87
|passage=The notion of two homosexuals living together more or less openly did not sit well with their neighbors, or even their friends, but Millthorpe took on a kind of symbolic '''luster''' as a kind of homosexual paradise.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|work=FT Weekend|date=2023-02-11|title=After Germany's fall, which is the paragon nation?|page=22|author=w:Janan Ganesh|passage=Where else then, Denmark? Its misgivings about immigration have smudged some of the liberal '''lustre''' it once had.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} [[polish|Polish]], [[social]] [[refinement]].
#: {{ux|en|Sure, the posh git spoke with fine '''lustre'''. ’S all a load of bollocks, though, innit?}}
# A [[thing]] [[exhibit]]ing luster, [[particularly]]
## {{lb|en|literary}} Any [[shining]] [[body]] or [[thing]].
## A [[piece]] of [[glass]] [[add]]ed to a [[light]] ([[especially]] a [[chandelier]]) to [[increase]] its luster.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1735|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=The First Satire of the Second Book of Horace Imitated|section=45-48
|passage=Each mortal has his pleasure: none deny<br>Scarsdale his bottle, Darty his ham-pie;<br>Ridotta sips and dances, till she see<br>The doubling '''lustres''' dance as fast as she;}}
## {{senseid|en|ornamental light}} An [[ornamental]] [[light]] [[providing]] luster, [[especially]] a [[chandelier]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1905|translator=H.T. Lowe-Porter|author=w:Thomas Mann|chapter=The Blood of the Walsungs|title=Death in Venice & Seven Other Stories|page=294|publisher=Vintage|year_published=1954|location=New York
|passage=The immense room was carpeted, the walls were covered with eighteenth-century panelling, and three electric '''lustres''' hung from the ceiling.}}
## A [[substance]] that [[impart]]s luster to a [[surface]], such as [[polish]], [[gloss]], [[plumbago]], [[glaze]], etc.
##* {{RQ:Joyce Dubliners|The Dead|pages=219–220|pageref=220|passage=Gabriel coloured as if he felt he had made a mistake and, without looking at her, kicked off his goloshes and flicked actively with his muffler at his patent-leather shoes. {{...}} When he had flicked '''lustre''' into his shoes he stood up and pulled his waistcoat down more tightly on his plump body.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Yuka Kadoi|title=Islamic Chinoiserie: The Art of Mongol Iran|publisher=Edinburgh University Press|page=52
|passage=Chinese themes are equally recognisable in the star-shaped and hexagonal tiles with either moulded relief or '''lustre'''-painted decoration, sometimes surrounded by an inscription border {{...}}}}
## The [[layer]] of an [[object]] that [[impart]]s luster, [[chiefly]] [[with regard to]] [[ceramics]].
## {{clipping of|en|lusterware|nodot=1}}: [[highly]] [[lustrous]] [[ceramics]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:Freya Stark|title=The Southern Gates of Arabia: A Journey in the Hadhramaut|location=Boston|publisher=E.P. Dutton|chapter=XXIII|page=253
|passage=The whole place was covered with fragments of pottery, mostly very rough, and difficult to identify as to date. Two small '''lustre''' shards belong to the ninth or tenth century and a green glaze resembles the output of the kilns found by Sir Aurel Stein on the coast of Makran.}}
## A [[kind]] of [[lustrous]] [[fabric]] with a [[wool]] [[weft]] and [[cotton]], [[linen]], or [[silk]] [[warp]], [[chiefly]] [[used]] for [[women]]'s [[dress]]es.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=w:Xavier Herbert|title=[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]|location=New York|publisher=D. Appleton-Century|year_published=1943|chapter=IX|page=143|url=https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb
|passage=Mrs. McLash was dressed for travelling. She wore a black '''lustre''' skirt that just exposed her broken button-boots {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[glory]], an [[act]] or [[thing]] that [[impart]]s [[fame]] or [[renown]].

