====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{lb|en|American spelling}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|now|_|rare}} To [[have]] luster, to [[gleam]], to [[shine]].
#* '''1729''', Richard Savage, ''The Wanderer'', Sect. iii, l. 326:
#*: What bloom, what brightness '''lusters''' o’er her cheeks!
# {{lb|en|intransitive|now|_|rare}} To [[gain]] luster, to [[become]] [[lustrous]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give luster, [[particularly]]
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[make]] [[illustrious]] or [[attractive]], to [[distinguish]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1644|author=John Maxwell|title=Sacro-Sancta Regum Majestas|page=17
|passage=Our Puritans have from hence learned to colour and '''lustre''' their ugly Treasons... with the cloake of Religion.}}
## To [[coat]] with a [[lustrous]] [[material]] or [[glaze]], to [[impart]] [[physical]] luster to an [[object]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1985|author=w:Nadine Gordimer|chapter=Sins of the Third Age|title=Something Out There|page=69|publisher=Penguin
|passage=Peter and Mania found a ''pensione'' whose view was of chestnut woods and a horizon looped by peaks '''lustred''' with last winter’s snow, distant in time as well as space.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[shed light on]], to [[illustrate]], to [[show]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|lustrate|nodot=1}}, [[particularly]]
## {{synonym of|en|purify|nodot=1}}, to [[ritually]] [[cleanse]] or [[renew]].
## {{synonym of|en|look|nodot=1}}, to [[look over]], to [[survey]].

